{
  "openapi": "3.0.1",
  "info": {
    "title": "Bilhikma Platform API",
    "description": "REST API for the Bilhikma learning platform: student registration and approval, entity/class/subject structure, protected lesson delivery, textbooks, live sessions, notifications and the administration dashboard.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Every response — success or failure — is wrapped in the same envelope: `{ \"data\": ..., \"message\": \"...\", \"status_code\": 0|1 }`. `status_code` is a logical flag (1 = success, 0 = failure) and is independent of the HTTP status, which always stays protocol-correct.\n- Lists return `data.items` plus `data.meta.pagination` (`count`, `current_page`, `per_page`, `total`). An empty list is a success with `items: []`, never an error.\n- Validation failures return HTTP 422 with `errors` — an object keyed by field name, each holding an array of message strings.\n- Send `Accept: application/json` on every request. Without it Laravel may answer with an HTML error page instead of the JSON envelope.\n- Send `Accept-Language: ar` or `en` to choose the language of `message` and `errors`. Arabic is the default for the mobile app.\n- Authenticated requests carry `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>`. Tokens are bound to one device: logging in elsewhere revokes the previous session, and the next request from it returns 401.\n\n## Getting started\n\n1. Import `bilhikma-api.postman_environment.json` and select it.\n2. Set `base_url` to your server.\n3. Run **Authentication → Log in (admin)** once. The access and refresh tokens\n   are captured automatically — nothing needs to be pasted by hand.\n4. Run **Authentication → Log in (student)** to work through the app endpoints.\n\nIds are captured as you go: listing entities stores `entity_id`, listing lessons\nstores `content_id`, and so on. Requests further down the collection reference\nthose variables, so a folder can be run top to bottom without editing anything.\n\n> The API allows one active session per account. Logging in as the student\n> revokes the admin session and vice versa, so keep the two tokens in separate\n> variables — which is exactly what this collection does\n> (`access_token` and `admin_token`).",
    "version": "1.0.0"
  },
